<project name="U1L3p6-Squiral" app="Snap! 11.0.8, https://snap.berkeley.edu" version="2"><notes></notes><thumbnail>data:image/png;base64,</thumbnail><scenes select="1"><scene name="U1L3p6-Squiral"><notes></notes><hidden></hidden><headers></headers><code></code><blocks><block-definition s="polygon, number of sides: %&apos;sides&apos; side length: %&apos;length&apos;" type="command" category="motion"><header></header><code></code><translations></translations><inputs><input type="%s"></input><input type="%s"></input></inputs><script><block s="doRepeat"><block var="sides"/><script><block s="forward"><block var="length"/></block><block s="turn"><block s="reportQuotient"><l>360</l><block var="sides"/></block></block></script></block></script></block-definition><block-definition s="nest squares, number: %&apos;number&apos;" type="command" category="motion"><header></header><code></code><translations></translations><inputs><input type="%s"></input></inputs><script><block s="doFor"><l>i</l><l>1</l><block var="number"/><script><custom-block s="polygon, number of sides: %s side length: %s"><l>4</l><block s="reportVariadicProduct"><list><l>10</l><block var="i"/></list></block></custom-block></script></block></script></block-definition><block-definition s="better nest squares, number: %&apos;number&apos; increment: %&apos;inc&apos;" type="command" category="motion"><header></header><code></code><translations></translations><inputs><input type="%s"></input><input type="%s"></input></inputs><script><block s="doFor"><l>i</l><block var="number"/><l>1</l><script><block s="down"></block><custom-block s="polygon, number of sides: %s side length: %s"><l>4</l><block s="reportVariadicProduct"><list><block var="i"/><block var="inc"/></list></block></custom-block><block s="up"></block><block s="forward"><block s="reportQuotient"><block var="inc"/><l>2</l></block></block><block s="turn"><l>90</l></block><block s="forward"><block s="reportQuotient"><block var="inc"/><l>2</l></block></block><block s="turnLeft"><l>90</l></block></script></block></script><scripts><comment x="191.33333333333334" y="130.79999999999998" w="238.66666666666666" collapsed="false">The key insight is that each new square has to be offset by /half/ the size increment, in each of two dimensions.  Then you have to work out the directions of the two angles; I found it easiest to visualize from the outside in (note the inputs to FOR), but it can work the other way too.</comment></scripts></block-definition></blocks><primitives></primitives><stage name="Stage" width="480" height="360" costume="0" color="255,255,255,1" tempo="60" threadsafe="false" penlog="false" volume="100" pan="0" lines="round" ternary="true" hyperops="true" codify="false" inheritance="true" sublistIDs="false" id="67"><pentrails>data:image/png;base64,</pentrails><costumes><list struct="atomic" id="68"></list></costumes><sounds><list struct="atomic" id="69"></list></sounds><variables></variables><blocks></blocks><scripts></scripts><sprites select="1"><sprite name="Sprite" idx="1" x="36.38700087930283" y="-56.23262629098991" heading="180" scale="1" volume="100" pan="0" rotation="1" draggable="true" costume="0" color="80,80,80,1" pen="tip" id="74"><costumes><list struct="atomic" id="75"></list></costumes><sounds><list struct="atomic" id="76"></list></sounds><blocks></blocks><variables></variables><scripts><script x="13.333333333333345" y="13.333333333333334"><block s="doFor"><l>i</l><l>1</l><l>15</l><script><block s="doSayFor"><block s="reportVariadicProduct"><list><l>2</l><block var="i"/></list></block><l>2</l></block></script><comment w="161.33333333333334" collapsed="false">6.1(a).  Compare this problem with the one on page 1.2.5 about rolling two dice, in which multiplying a value by 2 isn&apos;t good enough.</comment></block></script><script x="13.333333333333345" y="101.66666666666667"><block s="doFor"><l>i</l><l>1</l><l>1000</l><script><block s="forward"><block s="reportQuotient"><block var="i"/><l>100</l></block></block><block s="turn"><l>5</l></block></script><comment w="163.33333333333334" collapsed="false">6.2(e).  The key to this problem is to realize that even at the outer end of the spiral, each MOVE has to be tiny.  The specific numbers aren&apos;t important; in this script, the largest MOVE is 1000/100=10 steps.</comment></block></script><script x="13.333333333333345" y="213.99999999999997"><custom-block s="nest squares, number: %s"><l></l><comment w="53.333333333333336" collapsed="true">6.4</comment></custom-block></script><script x="13.333333333333345" y="248.00000000000003"><custom-block s="better nest squares, number: %s increment: %s"><l></l><l></l><comment w="53.333333333333336" collapsed="true">TIF 6.A</comment></custom-block></script><comment x="13.333333333333345" y="282" w="235.99999999999997" collapsed="true">TIF B-D in separate project U1L3p6-TIF-Albers</comment><comment x="13.333333333333345" y="309.3333333333333" w="393.3333333333333" collapsed="false">TIF 6.E (inspi).  The mathematics of the inspi is quite complicated.  Here&apos;s a reference that should be accessible to students:&#xD;https://bjc.edc.org/bjc-r/data/Rademacher-inspi.pdf</comment></scripts></sprite></sprites></stage><variables></variables></scene></scenes></project>